                        {
                          "id": "bfb959c3-2406-4b33-806e-24b80d5d0949",
                          "text": "签名: ∀ {α : Sort u} {a : α}, a = a",
                          "children": [],
                          "isExpanded": true,
                          "parentId": "f507d550-c261-41d1-81fa-07ac4c9f9704",
                          "data": {
                            "note": "**签名**\n\n∀ {α : Sort u} {a : α}, a = a",
                            "tags": [
                              "attribute",
                              "签名"
                            ]
                          },
                          "style": {
                            "background": "#f9fafb",
                            "color": "#6b7280",
                            "fontSize": 11,
                            "borderColor": "#e5e7eb"
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "id": "96bf1ded-c7fd-405d-922a-d3efd9dddfbc",
                          "text": "文档: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same …",
                          "children": [],
                          "isExpanded": true,
                          "parentId": "f507d550-c261-41d1-81fa-07ac4c9f9704",
                          "data": {
                            "note": "**文档**\n\n`rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the\nsame as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.\n\nThis is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although\nthe statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is\ndefinitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in\nLean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality.",
                            "tags": [
                              "attribute",
                              "文档"
                            ]
                          },
                          "style": {
                            "background": "#f9fafb",
                            "color": "#6b7280",
                            "fontSize": 11,
                            "borderColor": "#e5e7eb"
                          }
                        }
                      ],
                      "isExpanded": false,
                      "parentId": "ee3a78bf-a5bb-4741-a16b-2e5b80caecfa",
                      "data": {
                        "tags": [
                          "attribute-group"
                        ]
                      },
                      "style": {
                        "background": "#f3f4f6",
                        "color": "#4b5563",
                        "fontSize": 12,
                        "shape": "round",
                        "borderColor": "#d1d5db"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "isExpanded": true,
                  "parentId": "bdb92e99-5003-4a63-a9e9-21227516d6b0",
                  "data": {
                    "note": "**层** 证明\n\n**类型** definition\n\n**模块** `Init.Prelude`\n\n**签名**\n```lean\n∀ {α : Sort u} {a : α}, a = a\n```\n\n**文档**\n`rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the\nsame as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.\n\nThis is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although\nthe statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is\ndefinitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in\nLean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality.",
                    "tags": [
                      "declaration",
                      "证明",
                      "definition"
                    ]
                  },
                  "style": {
                    "background": "#f7fef9",
                    "color": "#14532d",
