                        {
                          "id": "b120eaf4-98b3-4357-9d5e-7ebf794003b4",
                          "text": "签名: ∀ {α : Sort u} {β : α → Sort v} {f g : (x : α) → β x}, (∀ (x : α), f x = g x) →…",
                          "children": [],
                          "isExpanded": true,
                          "parentId": "5cf4256a-bdcc-46eb-a7e1-ac84a6afc16e",
                          "data": {
                            "note": "**签名**\n\n∀ {α : Sort u} {β : α → Sort v} {f g : (x : α) → β x}, (∀ (x : α), f x = g x) → f = g",
                            "tags": [
                              "attribute",
                              "签名"
                            ]
                          },
                          "style": {
                            "background": "#f9fafb",
                            "color": "#6b7280",
                            "fontSize": 11,
                            "borderColor": "#e5e7eb"
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "id": "f85c9b2d-1b45-40b0-b05f-6de82fd151b9",
                          "text": "文档: **Function extensionality.** If two functions return equal results for all poss…",
                          "children": [],
                          "isExpanded": true,
                          "parentId": "5cf4256a-bdcc-46eb-a7e1-ac84a6afc16e",
                          "data": {
                            "note": "**文档**\n\n**Function extensionality.** If two functions return equal results for all possible arguments, then\nthey are equal.\n\nIt is called “extensionality” because it provides a way to prove two objects equal based on the\nproperties of the underlying mathematical functions, rather than based on the syntax used to denote\nthem. Function extensionality is a theorem that can be [proved using quotient\ntypes](lean-manual://section/quotient-funext).",
                            "tags": [
                              "attribute",
                              "文档"
                            ]
                          },
                          "style": {
                            "background": "#f9fafb",
                            "color": "#6b7280",
                            "fontSize": 11,
                            "borderColor": "#e5e7eb"
                          }
                        }
                      ],
                      "isExpanded": false,
                      "parentId": "a9ccf099-1c80-4c94-a13f-f4d99b978d9f",
                      "data": {
                        "tags": [
                          "attribute-group"
                        ]
                      },
                      "style": {
                        "background": "#f3f4f6",
                        "color": "#4b5563",
                        "fontSize": 12,
                        "shape": "round",
                        "borderColor": "#d1d5db"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "isExpanded": true,
                  "parentId": "c6e271d6-ec64-4023-acfa-9f9637879305",
                  "data": {
                    "note": "**层** 证明\n\n**类型** theorem\n\n**模块** `Init.Core`\n\n**签名**\n```lean\n∀ {α : Sort u} {β : α → Sort v} {f g : (x : α) → β x}, (∀ (x : α), f x = g x) → f = g\n```\n\n**文档**\n**Function extensionality.** If two functions return equal results for all possible arguments, then\nthey are equal.\n\nIt is called “extensionality” because it provides a way to prove two objects equal based on the\nproperties of the underlying mathematical functions, rather than based on the syntax used to denote\nthem. Function extensionality is a theorem that can be [proved using quotient\ntypes](lean-manual://section/quotient-funext).",
                    "tags": [
                      "declaration",
                      "证明",
                      "theorem"
                    ]
                  },
                  "style": {
                    "background": "#f7fef9",
                    "color": "#14532d",
                    "fontSize": 13,
                    "borderColor": "#86efac"
                  }
