                        {
                          "id": "122d3ded-23d0-4931-b918-d185835ad9e7",
                          "text": "文档: Low-level quotient types. Quotient types coarsen the propositional equality for…",
                          "children": [],
                          "isExpanded": true,
                          "parentId": "4752fccf-caaf-4ad5-b309-634daee74224",
                          "data": {
                            "note": "**文档**\n\nLow-level quotient types. Quotient types coarsen the propositional equality for a type `α`, so that\nterms related by some relation `r` are considered equal in `Quot r`.\n\nSet-theoretically, `Quot r` can be seen as the set of equivalence classes of `α` modulo `r`.\nFunctions from `Quot r` must prove that they respect `r`: to define a function `f : Quot r → β`,\nit is necessary to provide `f' : α → β` and prove that for all `x : α` and `y : α`,\n`r x y → f' x = f' y`.\n\n`Quot` is a built-in primitive:\n * `Quot.mk` places elements of the underlying type `α` into the quotient.\n * `Quot.lift` allows the definition of functions from the quotient to some other type.\n * `Quot.sound` asserts the equality of elements related by `r`.\n * `Quot.ind` is used to write proofs about quotients by assuming that all elements are constructed\n   with `Quot.mk`.\n\nThe relation `r` is not required to be an equivalence relation; the resulting quotient type's\nequality extends `r` to an equivalence as a consequence of the rules for equality and quotients.\nWhen `r` is an equivalence relation, it can be more convenient to use the higher-level type\n`Quotient`.",
                            "tags": [
                              "attribute",
                              "文档"
                            ]
                          },
                          "style": {
                            "background": "#f9fafb",
                            "color": "#6b7280",
                            "fontSize": 11,
                            "borderColor": "#e5e7eb"
                          }
                        }
                      ],
                      "isExpanded": false,
                      "parentId": "b6acad82-2820-406f-89ed-eeb3a7f4fe45",
                      "data": {
                        "tags": [
                          "attribute-group"
                        ]
                      },
                      "style": {
                        "background": "#f3f4f6",
                        "color": "#4b5563",
                        "fontSize": 12,
                        "shape": "round",
                        "borderColor": "#d1d5db"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "isExpanded": true,
                  "parentId": "fbeb1a68-70ad-4f3a-bbdb-6b6210739c89",
                  "data": {
                    "note": "**层** 证明\n\n**类型** quotient\n\n**模块** `Init.Prelude`\n\n**签名**\n```lean\n{α : Sort u} → (α → α → Prop) → Sort u\n```\n\n**文档**\nLow-level quotient types. Quotient types coarsen the propositional equality for a type `α`, so that\nterms related by some relation `r` are considered equal in `Quot r`.\n\nSet-theoretically, `Quot r` can be seen as the set of equivalence classes of `α` modulo `r`.\nFunctions from `Quot r` must prove that they respect `r`: to define a function `f : Quot r → β`,\nit is necessary to provide `f' : α → β` and prove that for all `x : α` and `y : α`,\n`r x y → f' x = f' y`.\n\n`Quot` is a built-in primitive:\n * `Quot.mk` places elements of the underlying type `α` into the quotient.\n * `Quot.lift` allows the definition of functions from the quotient to some other type.\n * `Quot.sound` asserts the equality of elements related by `r`.\n * `Quot.ind` is used to write proofs about quotients by assuming that all elements are constructed\n   with `Quot.mk`.\n\nThe relation `r` is not required to be an equivalence relation; the resulting quotient type's\nequality extends `r` to an equivalence as a consequence of the rules for equality and quotients.\nWhen `r` is an equivalence relation, it can be more convenient to use the higher-level type\n`Quotient`.",
                    "tags": [
                      "declaration",
                      "证明",
                      "quotient"
                    ]
                  },
                  "style": {
                    "background": "#f7fef9",
                    "color": "#14532d",
                    "fontSize": 13,
                    "borderColor": "#86efac"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "b12fac58-6538-45ef-a0de-3883a332692c",
                  "text": "[证明] Quot.liftOn",
                  "children": [
                    {
